/* ==============================================================
   06 — Time-based motion
   --------------------------------------------------------------
   ROBOT     MCS500 only (R1)
   TEACHES   SetMoveMode, SetMoveDuration, SetMoveDurationCfg
   NEEDS     Robot activated, no tool mounted
   WATCH     Four moves of very different lengths, all taking
             the same time
   TIME      ~30 s
   ============================================================== */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   A DIFFERENT QUESTION

   Every example so far has asked "how fast should the robot go?"
   and let the duration fall out of that.

   Sometimes the requirement is the other way round. A machine
   indexes every 1.2 seconds. A camera exposes on a fixed
   interval. Two axes have to arrive together. In those cases you
   do not care about velocity — you care that the move takes
   as long as it is supposed to.

       SetMoveMode(m)

     m = 0   velocity-based (default). SetJointVel,
             SetCartLinVel and SetCartAngVel govern.
     m = 1   time-based. SetMoveDuration governs, and the
             velocity settings are ignored.

       SetMoveDuration(t)     t in seconds; default 3

   In time-based mode the robot computes whatever velocity
   profile makes the move take t seconds. A 5 mm move and a
   200 mm move both take t. The short one crawls, the long one
   hurries, and both land on the beat.

   One precision from the manual: the duration you set does not
   include the acceleration and deceleration ramps — they come on
   top. Time a move once if you need the exact total.

   WHY THIS IS USEFUL

     - Synchronising to an external cycle: the robot's move is
       the same length every time, so the machine's timing does
       not depend on where the part happened to be.
     - Smoothness: a long slow move at a computed constant pace
       is gentler than one clipped by a velocity limit.
     - Predictable cycle time, which is what a customer actually
       budgets in — not peak velocity.

   WHAT IF IT CANNOT?

   You can ask for a duration that is physically impossible. What
   happens then is your choice:

       SetMoveDurationCfg(s)

     s = 0   silent, do it as fast as possible
     s = 1   log a warning saying the shortest possible duration
     s = 4   raise error [3051], also naming the shortest
             possible duration                        (default)

   Severity 1 is the useful one during development: the warning
   tells you the fastest the move can actually go, which is
   exactly the number you need to choose a realistic duration.
   The default of 4 is the right production setting — if the
   machine's timing cannot be met, you want to know loudly, not
   quietly drift out of sync.

   Note that SetMoveDuration(0) means "as fast as possible", but
   only when the severity is 0 or 1.

   And the time-scaling slider still applies on top: at 50%,
   every move takes twice as long, whatever SetMoveDuration
   says. The robot checks your durations against the unscaled
   numbers, so a program does not have to change to be run
   slowly.

   THE ONE COMMAND THAT MUST STAY OUT

   MoveJump is not supported in time-based mode, and the manual
   says it must not be used there. If a program mixes the two,
   switch back with SetMoveMode(0) before the first jump. As
   always, these settings return to their defaults on activation.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* --- Standard reset: start from a known state -----------------
   Deactivating loses every NON-persistent setting — tool and
   world frames, speeds, accelerations, blending, payload, torque
   limits, posture and turn selection, move mode, MoveJump
   settings — so this program starts from firmware defaults no
   matter what ran before it. Reactivating brings the motors
   back.

   This does NOT move the arm: the MCS500 has absolute encoders
   and never needs homing, which is why there is no Home() here.

   It does NOT reset persistent settings — joint limits, work
   zone, tool sphere, collision, P-Stop 2 and calibration config
   all survive. Run 00_common/00_cold_start.mxprog for those.

   This block is identical in every example. To remove it
   everywhere, see the README. */
DeactivateRobot()
ActivateRobot()
ResetError()
ResumeMotion()


MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)
Delay(1)


/* --- Velocity-based: the default -------------------------------
   Four moves of increasing length. Time them roughly — each one
   takes noticeably longer than the last, because they all run at
   the same speed over different distances. */

SetMoveMode(0)
SetCartLinVel(100)

MovePose(170, 0, 80, 0)
Delay(0.5)

MoveLin(170, 10, 80, 0)                // 10 mm
Delay(0.5)
MoveLin(170, -30, 80, 0)               // 40 mm
Delay(0.5)
MoveLin(170, 60, 80, 0)                // 90 mm
Delay(0.5)
MoveLin(170, -60, 80, 0)               // 120 mm
Delay(1.5)


/* --- Time-based: every move takes 1.5 s ------------------------
   Identical destinations. Now watch the clock instead of the
   robot: the 10 mm move and the 120 mm move take the same time.

   The short move is visibly, deliberately slow. That is not the
   robot struggling — it is the robot hitting a deadline. */

SetMoveMode(1)

// Warn rather than error if a duration cannot be met, so this
// file runs to the end and tells us the shortest achievable time
// in the log panel.
SetMoveDurationCfg(1)

SetMoveDuration(1.5)

MovePose(170, 0, 80, 0)
Delay(0.5)

MoveLin(170, 10, 80, 0)                // 10 mm, in 1.5 s
Delay(0.5)
MoveLin(170, -30, 80, 0)               // 40 mm, in 1.5 s
Delay(0.5)
MoveLin(170, 60, 80, 0)                // 90 mm, in 1.5 s
Delay(0.5)
MoveLin(170, -60, 80, 0)               // 120 mm, in 1.5 s
Delay(1.5)


/* --- Asking for the impossible ---------------------------------
   20 milliseconds for a 120 mm move is not going to happen. With
   severity 1 the robot moves as fast as it can and logs a
   warning naming the shortest duration it could actually
   achieve.

   Watch the log panel for that number — during development it is
   the most useful output this feature produces. */

SetMoveDuration(0.02)
MoveLin(170, 60, 80, 0)

Delay(1)


// --- Back to velocity mode and home ---------------------------
SetMoveMode(0)
SetMoveDurationCfg(4)
SetCartLinVel(150)
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THINGS TO TRY

   1. Run the two blocks back to back with a stopwatch on the
      screen. Four moves, four different distances, one duration.
      It is a strange thing to watch and it sticks.

   2. This feature only sells to people with a timing problem —
      indexing machines, camera-synchronised inspection, motion
      that has to match a conveyor. Ask whether anything in their
      cycle has to happen on a fixed beat. If nothing does, move
      on; if something does, this is often the whole reason they
      choose the robot.

   3. The impossible-duration warning is worth showing to an
      engineer. A controller that tells you the fastest it could
      have gone is a controller someone thought about.

   NEXT   07_singularities.mxprog — the one place on a SCARA
          where the arm runs out of directions.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */
